+# Voice
+
+Read this before writing any sentence a reader will see. It sits beside `IR_EDITORIAL_STYLE_GUIDE.md`, which owns page shapes and the name table. This file owns how a sentence sounds. `npm run copy:ratchet` enforces the parts a machine can check.
+
+## The reader and the speaker
+
+The speaker is an analyst who has read the reader's answers and has a view. The analyst says what the answers show, names the case that tests it, and says what would change the call. The analyst does not describe the product, its review status, or its limits unless the reader is about to act on something those limits affect.
+
+## Guardrails limit the claim, not the sentence
+
+AGENTS.md asks for no fake precision and no claim the model cannot support. Meet that by choosing a smaller claim and stating it plainly. Do not state a larger claim and then deny it.
+
+> Before: "This is a model result. It does not establish a durable trait."
+> After: delete it. "Your answers put rivalry first" is already scoped to answers.
+
+> Before: "It does not mean 54% of people agree with you, and it is not a percentile."
+> After: "5.4 is where your answers sit on this dimension's 1 to 7 scale."
+
+If a sentence starts with "This is not", "It does not", "No … is assigned", or "These are authored", rewrite it as what the thing is.
+
+## Report, then interpret once
+
+First say what the person answered. That needs no hedge because it is a fact about their answers. Then give one interpretation, once, under a plain label. Do not qualify each sentence of the interpretation. One scope sentence at the top of a section is enough; after that, commit.
+
+> "You agreed strongly with both rivalry statements and disagreed with both rules statements. Read as: you expect a rival to take what it can, so you want the cost of trying to be visible early."
+
+## Know who is reading
+
+| Page | Who reads it | Person |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Result, owner's first view | The person who answered | "you", about their answers |
+| Result opened from a share link | Someone else | "these answers", "this reader" |
+| Archetype, Library, Decision Pattern, Thinker | Anyone, often before answering | third person about the idea: "Kairos puts power and timing first" |
+| Quiz item and explainer | The person answering | "you" only in the question itself |
+
+Never write "your answers", "your result", or "in this result" on a page a visitor can reach without answering. That copy also becomes the search snippet.
+
+## One limit per screen
+
+A screen may carry one sentence about a limit, placed next to the action it affects (a share button, a delete button, a result that cannot be shown). Everything else about limits goes to Method, once, stated as what the method does.
+
+## Explainers show the two answers
+
+A question's explainer never restates the question. It has three parts, each optional:
+
+- Agree if: one sentence describing what someone who agrees believes about a concrete case.
+- Disagree if: the same for disagreement.
+- Term: a definition for one technical term.
+
+Use one case for both sides, so the illustration shows the choice without tilting it. An explainer may not add a condition to the claim or illustrate only one side, because people who open it would then be answering a different question from people who do not.
+
+## Anchor in something that happened
+
+Prefer a named case, date, document, or actor over an abstraction. "Kennedy's quarantine in October 1962" does more than "an escalation ceiling". Two anchors per paragraph on reference pages, one per result section.
+
+## End on the call and what would flip it
+
+A result or reference section ends with a judgment and the condition that would change it. "What would change this" replaces "What this does not claim".
+
+## Mechanics
+
+- Twelve to twenty-two words per sentence. A full stop beats a semicolon.
+- No em dashes. No "not X but Y", "X, not Y", "rather than" unless both halves carry information.
+- At most one negation per sentence. Two is a rewrite.
+- Break any three-item parallel list that exists for rhythm.
+- No sentence whose subject is the product, a record, a layer, a form, a version, or a model.
+- One name per thing, from the style guide's table. Never render a family label ("Strategic Realist") or a technical descriptor ("Bridge Builder") next to the public name.
+
+## Brief for a model writing copy
+
+Paste this with the file list:
+
+```
+Write the reader-facing copy in [files]. Read docs/voice/VOICE.md and IR_EDITORIAL_STYLE_GUIDE.md first.
+Say what the answers show, then interpret once. Choose claims the data supports and state them without disclaimers.
+Do not add sentences about review status, validation, versions, storage, or what the model does not claim.
+Third person on any page a visitor can reach without answering.
+Run npm run copy:ratchet. It must pass. Do not satisfy it with synonyms: if a sentence trips negation-stack or audit-voice, change what the sentence claims.
+Deliver the diff and docs/review/after-passages-[task].md with before/after pairs. Nothing else.
+```
